.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH rqx 1  "rqx " 
.SH NAME
rqx \- Finds dead weight in TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go, Java and C++ and says why it matters
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBrqx\fR [\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR] [\fB\-\-motion\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR] [\fB\-\-progress\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fIsubcommands\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Finds dead weight in TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go, Java and C++ and says why it matters
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR
Never open the interactive interface; bare `rqx` prints the report instead
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-mouse\fR
Leave the mouse to the terminal. The session never captures it; the flag is still accepted so an existing invocation keeps working
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fI<COLOR>\fR [default: auto]
When to colour output; `auto` follows NO_COLOR and whether stdout is a terminal
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto
.IP \(bu 2
always
.IP \(bu 2
never
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-motion\fR \fI<MOTION>\fR [default: on]
Animation in the interactive interface
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
on
.IP \(bu 2
off
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-progress\fR
Do not draw the progress bar on stderr; `\-\-quiet` implies it
.TP
\fB\-\-progress\fR \fI<PROGRESS>\fR [default: auto]
How a scan reports progress on stderr; `plain` prints a line every two seconds for a job log
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
auto: A bar on a terminal, nothing in CI or when stderr is redirected
.IP \(bu 2
bar: A bar whenever stderr is a terminal, CI included
.IP \(bu 2
plain: A line on stderr every two seconds and at each phase, for a job log
.IP \(bu 2
none: Nothing
.RE
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Say more: \-v adds the configuration, the cache and every skipped file to the report; \-vv also prints stage timings and git commands on stderr
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
rqx\-scan(1)
Analyze a project and report every finding
.TP
rqx\-fix(1)
Apply the fixes a finding decides on its own, proving each one before it is written. Previews by default
.TP
rqx\-verify(1)
Compare the working tree against a base revision and say what happened to each finding: resolved, improved, relocated, or introduced
.TP
rqx\-setup(1)
Print the configuration one editor needs to talk to rqx, or list them all
.TP
rqx\-plan(1)
Rank the work by what it buys against what it costs, and say what the health score would be after it
.TP
rqx\-explain(1)
Print a rule\*(Aqs or a pattern\*(Aqs taxonomy entry: what it maps to, what decides it, and what fixes it
.TP
rqx\-rules(1)
List every rule Rqx can report, grouped by family
.TP
rqx\-baseline(1)
Record the findings this project already has, so a gate can judge only what a change adds
.TP
rqx\-suppressions(1)
List every finding a comment in the source waives, with the reason given
.TP
rqx\-complexity(1)
What the analyser measured, as a distribution rather than only its tail
.TP
rqx\-perf(1)
Where the work grows faster than the data, ranked by reach
.TP
rqx\-update(1)
Install the newest release, or say what it would change
.TP
rqx\-completions(1)
Print a shell completion script
.TP
rqx\-recommend(1)
What to do about this project, as distinct from what is wrong with it
.TP
rqx\-slop(1)
Which files read as written rather than read
.TP
rqx\-health(1)
Where the health score comes from, and what removing each part would buy
.TP
rqx\-graph(1)
Print the import graph — as text, Graphviz, Mermaid or data
.TP
rqx\-cache(1)
Show what the analysis cache holds, or throw it away
.TP
rqx\-query(1)
Run a tree\-sitter query over a project, to try one out before writing it into a `[[rule]]`
.TP
rqx\-techniques(1)
List every refactoring Rqx prescribes, and which rules ask for it
.TP
rqx\-levels(1)
Print what each cleanliness level changes, as the settings it would take to write it by hand
.TP
rqx\-patterns(1)
Report which design patterns a project uses, which are half applied, and where one would fit
.TP
rqx\-check(1)
Analyze changed files against a base revision and fail if the gate is not met
.TP
rqx\-comment(1)
Post the review onto the merge request it is reviewing, and grade the commit
.TP
rqx\-init(1)
Write a commented rqx.yaml with every setting at its default
.TP
rqx\-config(1)
Print the settings a run will use, and the file each one came from
.TP
rqx\-mcp(1)
Run as an MCP server, so an AI agent can ask what a project\*(Aqs problems are
.TP
rqx\-lsp(1)
Run as a Language Server over stdio, for editor integration
